  "note": "From Inkscape's official \"Inkscape keyboard and mouse reference\" (version 1.4.x, last revised 2024-10-05), which mirrors share/inkscape/keys/inkscape.xml. Only the keyboard entries are kept; the reference's mouse entries (click, drag, double-click, right-click, middle-click and mouse-wheel bindings, and every \"modifier + drag\" row) are left out because the chord grammar cannot express them. Also left out: rows whose key is \"arrows\" or \"Ctrl+arrows\" and similar, which name four keys at once; every \"keypad N\" row (the Display/Color mode, Split Canvas, X-Ray Mode and Align sections are keypad-only); the \"Left Ctrl\" / \"Right Ctrl\" / \"Left Alt\" / \"Right Alt\" node-handle rows, which distinguish the physical left and right modifier; and rows whose key is a character outside the grammar's key set (* ! ? # % | & ^ ( ) { } < >), which drops for example union (Ctrl+plus is kept where it is the listed key), \"invert selection\" (!), \"open command palette\" (?), \"toggle grids visibility\" (#), \"toggle snapping\" (%) and the inset/outset rows. Where the reference lists several equivalent keys for one action, the first expressible one is kept (so \"s, F1 - Selector\" becomes S). Section names are used as context for the tool and dialog sections; the application-wide sections (Tools, Canvas, Document, Palette, File, Window, Extensions, Layers, Object, Path) use an empty context, except where that would make two actions share a name, in which case the section name is used. One row, \"Pen (Bezier) tool\", is printed as \"B+Shift+F6\" where every other tool row reads \"<letter>, <F-key>\"; it is read as the two alternatives B and Shift+F6 and only B is kept. The reference documents one set of chords and gives no separate Mac ones, so only `windows` is filled in.",
